                              CHAPTER SIX


                            The Circle
             T R AC K   “Manteca” — Dizzy Gillespie and His Orchestra
                             ECHO    the clearing, 1991




Manny sent me a voice memo at 9:03 Sunday morning. It was my own
voice saying “Yeah,” slowed down and looped, with a conga under it.
The caption read NOW GREMLIN.
    I almost didn’t go. I had reasons, and they were good ones: the mur‐
al, the EP, a Thursday appointment I was already half-composing an
excuse to cancel, a body that had slept maybe ninety minutes and was
holding a grudge. But I’d said yes at a curb, on tape, in front of a wit‐
ness, and I’m a man of my word when someone is recording.
    Mamá caught me at the door anyway. She didn’t ask where I was
going. She pressed a warm foil package into my hands and tucked a
napkin into my pocket on which she’d written one word in her slanted
capitals: EAT. Under it, smaller: y vuelve. And come back. I was twenty-
five years old, and I put the napkin in my wallet.
    It was the first Sunday of the year that smelled like dirt instead of
salt.
    The circle meets at the north end of the park, past the handball
courts, in a clearing under the big oaks that no official ever claimed, so
the neighborhood claimed it. Around it runs a low stone ring from the
thirties, the kind of public-works bench that was built to outlast who‐
ever commissioned it. I almost made it to the clearing without incident.
    Then I passed a guy handing out flyers.



